It’s Sunday morning in a church in Any Town, USA. The sanctuary is full. The worship band plays heartfelt songs of praise as the congregation sings along, hands raised and voices united. Pastors stand at the front, leading powerful prayers and reading scripture. Together they declare the Apostles’ Creed. Joyful “Hallelujahs!” and “Amens!” fill the room.\
\
**Then Monday arrives.**

It’s easy to see this pattern in any church. But if we’re honest, we do the same thing. The cozy feeling of community disappears. Compromise feels easier than obedience. Our patience with the kids wears thin by Wednesday. Conversations with our spouses grow distant. The habits we laid aside on Sunday call again.

Before we know it, calendars fill up, notifications never stop, and the moments we intended for God just simply disappear. ***“Real life”*** has returned, and our hearts feel thin by mid-week.

We, too, are sometimes living a Hosea 6:4 kind of life.

“Your love is like a morning cloud,\
like the dew that goes early away.” (Hosea 6:4 ESV)

One of my favorite hymns has always been, *Come Thou Fount of Every Blessing.*&nbsp; It sings what most of us would rather hide: that we are "Prone to wander." If I am being honest, that’s me more than I’d like to admit.\
\
In Hosea 6 and 7, Hosea shows us what that wandering looks like and what God thinks of it.&nbsp;

## God Grieves

God sees it all. He sees the full sanctuary, the passionate singing, and the heartfelt prayers on Sunday. But He also sees what happens on Monday, Tuesday, and beyond. Scripture tells us it grieves Him.

In Hosea’s day, Israel was doing well. Temples were busy. Sacrifices continued. The economy was strong. Everything looked just fine on the outside.

But God saw the truth: their love disappeared almost as fast as it appeared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

We’ve read this repeatedly in the Old Testament; this was a cycle Israel repeated for generations. They would cry out for rescue, receive God’s mercy, and then forget His deliverance, like a child who says “sorry” just to get out of trouble.

We see the same thing here in Hosea 6. The people sang what sounded like a sincere song of return:

“Come, let us return to the Lord;\
for he has torn us, that he may heal us;\
he has struck us down, and he will bind us up.\
\
After two days he will revive us;\
on the third day he will raise us up,\
that we may live before him.” (Hosea 6:1-2)

It sounded good. It probably even felt good.

But there was no real confession of sin. No brokenness. No lasting change. Just a quick religious high, patting themselves on the back for “returning to God”.

Their love was like dew on the grass: pretty for a moment, then gone before the sun rose.

The rest of the chapter gets even heavier: Hosea piles image upon image. The priests were like robbers lying in wait. The nation’s passion burned like an overheated oven. And my personal favorite is Hosea’s description of Israel as “a cake not turned”, burned to a crisp on one side and completely raw on the other.&nbsp; Every time I read that image, I kind of smile and then I realize Hosea isn't really trying to be funny. He's showing us what divided hearts really look like.

Every picture pointed to the same reality: beneath all their religious activity, their hearts were far from God.

Israel was fluttering around like a “silly dove”, flying back and forth between Egypt and Assyria, looking for help anywhere except from God. Instead of trusting the God who loved them with steadfast ***hesed***, they kept putting their hope in the military power of two nations that actually hated them.

They trusted what looked powerful instead of the One who had always been faithful.

But perhaps the hardest part? God says they had “transgressed the covenant” (Hosea 6:7). They continued with their worship practices, ignoring the fact that they had broken their promises to Him.

You can hear the heartbreak in His voice as He cries out:

“What shall I do with you, O Ephraim?\
What shall I do with you, O Judah?” (Hosea 6:4)

He wanted to heal them (7:1). He was ready to restore them. But Israel kept turning away.

## God Desires

But then (always a good transition, right?), God reveals his heart through Hosea. Hosea shares the words that go straight to the heart, the very words Jesus Himself would later quote in Matthew:

“I desire hesed and not sacrifice,\
the knowledge of God rather than burnt offerings.” (Hosea 6:6)

**So what is hesed?**

It’s loyal, steadfast, covenant-keeping love that God has always longed for from His people. The kind that keeps its promises even when the other person doesn’t.

It’s love that shows up day after day, not just when it feels good. Love that stays. Even when it’s hard, even when you’re tired, even when no one’s watching.

Think of it like Noah in *The Notebook*. After decades of marriage, Alzheimer’s steals Allie’s memory. She no longer knows who he is. Yet Noah visits her daily, reads their story, and continues to love her because of a promise, not because she can return his love and devotion. Let’s just admit it here, we all want someone who loves us like that.&nbsp; Funny thing is we already do.

That’s hesed. Gritty. Faithful. Stubbornly committed. It’s the same love God had for Israel and Christ has for us.

Our services and our liturgy are beautiful and meaningful. God delights in sincere worship. He is not against the songs, the prayers, or the heartfelt gathering of His people.

But He is saying something important here: all our Sunday beauty and meaning can become empty if it is not joined to loyal hearts during the week.

He wants more than religious activity…He wants us to truly know Him in a deep, everyday relationship.

## God Pursues

Even while grieving their unfaithfulness, God reveals His own heart: “When I would heal Israel…” (Hosea 7:1).

The God who grieves over our fickle love never stops pursuing us with His steadfast hesed.

Hosea doesn’t say this is the end of the story.&nbsp; In fact, as we saw earlier, Hosea actually points us to Jesus, where God's pursuing hesed finds its ultimate expression.

**So how do we move from dew-like devotion to lasting hesed?** Here are five things we can do to move towards lasting hesed. And for full disclosure, these aren’t five easy steps I’ve mastered or am consistent with. Most weeks I’m still fighting to do even one of them because life gets busy and I make choices. Life gets loud, I get distracted, and I am “prone to wander” and choose the easier thing far more often than I want to admit.

\****\
**Run the Dew Test.** Notice when your faith feels strongest and when it quietly fades.

**Pursue Knowing God.** Spend time daily in Scripture and prayer, not to check a box, but to deepen your relationship with Him.

**Let Sunday Shape Monday.** Let worship overflow into your work, your marriage, your parenting, and your conversations.

**Confess and Return.** Name your sin honestly before God instead of hiding behind excuses.

**Stay in Community.** Walk with believers who will encourage you toward faithfulness when your love grows cold.

Hosea shows us a God who sees it all, both the packed sanctuary and the scattered, half-hearted hearts. And yet God still refuses to walk away from us.

He sees every place where our love grows thin.

And through it all, He keeps pursuing us with the very ***hesed*** we keep failing to give back.

The question He asked Israel is the same one ringing over your life and mine today:

***Will you keep giving me morning mist that disappears with the sun, or will you finally give me the steadfast love I have always desired?***

The choice is ours.

The pursuit is His.&nbsp;

*Lord, help us become that people.*
